{# The About window. On a desktop browser it is spawned from the Apple menu, the desktop icon or the Application menu (see windows.js), so a template copy is used; on a phone it is the page at /about/. #}
{% set hl = "h1" if static else "h2" %}
<section class="window about" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
  <div class="titlebar">
    {% if static %}<span class="closebox" aria-hidden="true"></span>{% else %}<button type="button" class="closebox" aria-label="Close About This Collection"></button>{% endif %}
    <{{ hl }} id="about-title">About This Collection</{{ hl }}>
    <span class="zoombox" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="winbody">
  <div class="vscroll" data-vscroll>
  <div class="vs-view" data-vs-view tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="About This Collection">
  <div class="doc prose">
    <p>I collect vintage Macintosh computers, keep them running, and write down what they are. This site is the shelf where all of it lives. It has three jobs: preserving the machines, archiving what they look like and what is inside them, and having fun with them.</p>

    <h2>Preserving</h2>
    <p>Old Macs do not last by themselves. Capacitors leak, batteries die, hard drives wear out, and plastics turn brittle. I try to keep each machine working, using the operating system it was made for wherever I can. Some have had a worn-out hard drive swapped for a modern solid-state card on an adapter, and some are waiting on a repair. When a machine does not work, its page says so and says what I know about why, because a machine that is documented as broken is easier to fix later than one that was never recorded.</p>

    <h2>Archiving</h2>
    <p>Every machine gets its own page, listed from oldest to newest in <a href="{{ '/' | url }}">the collection</a>. Each one has photos, the specifications as the machine itself reports them (usually from System Profiler or About This Computer), and a plain note on its condition. Where I have filled in a detail from Apple's published documentation instead of the machine, the page says so. Serial numbers and anything personal are blurred out of the photos. The aim is a record that is accurate enough to trust and that someone else could use when they are trying to identify, repair, or restore the same model.</p>

    <h2>Having fun</h2>
    <p>These machines were built to be used, and a lot of the joy is in using them: bringing one back to life, seeing it run the software of its own era, and filming it. The <a href="{{ '/videos/' | url }}">montage videos</a> come from that.</p>
    <p>If you collect, restore, or just remember these computers, I hope you find something here worth looking at.</p>

    <h2>Credits</h2>
    <p>The look of this site was guided by the Mac OS 9 screenshots on <a href="https://guidebookgallery.org/screenshots/macos90/">GUIdebook</a>, a gallery of graphical user interfaces created by Marcin Wichary. Thank you to GUIdebook for keeping them online.</p>
  </div>
  </div>
  <div class="vbar" data-vs-bar hidden>
    <button type="button" class="vbtn" data-dir="-1" aria-label="Scroll up" tabindex="-1">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" shape-rendering="crispEdges" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><polygon points="8,4 13,10 3,10" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="vtrack" data-vs-track><span class="vknob" data-vs-knob></span></div>
    <button type="button" class="vbtn" data-dir="1" aria-label="Scroll down" tabindex="-1">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" shape-rendering="crispEdges" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><polygon points="8,12 13,6 3,6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
    </button>
  </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>
